## Restore

{% if node_seed_copied | bool %}
1. Provision the host. Put it in the same groups.
2. Copy `secret.seed` to `{{ node_seed_path }}` **before the first start**:

```bash
scp secret.seed {{ ansible_host | default(inventory_hostname) }}:/tmp/secret.seed
ssh {{ ansible_host | default(inventory_hostname) }} \
  'install -d -o {{ crosslink_user }} -g {{ crosslink_user }} -m 700 {{ node_seed_path | dirname }} &&
   install -o {{ crosslink_user }} -g {{ crosslink_user }} -m 600 \
     /tmp/secret.seed {{ node_seed_path }} && rm /tmp/secret.seed'
```

3. Run the playbook.

The seed's directory is named by the release for the season (`zebrad/src/config.rs`). A release that renames it starts the node with a fresh seed, a new identity and a new wallet. Before upgrading, read the name in the new release and copy the seed there first. The playbook never overwrites this bundle's seed with a different one: a run after such a start stops and says which seed is which.

{% if crosslink_finalizer_enabled | bool %}
This seed is a finalizer identity. Restore it onto one host at a time, and stop the old one first. Two running nodes on one identity is equivocation.
{% else %}
Never restore a seed onto two running hosts if it belongs to a finalizer. That is equivocation. For the nodes here it is harmless.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
Provision the host, put it in the same groups, run the playbook. Nothing to restore: the chain state resyncs and the seed is regenerated.
{% endif %}
